+This methods note reports what one instrument can and cannot see of
+conversational tone: cached keys projected on emotion directions at four
+early full-attention layers. Its readings move with sequence position
+and the amount of history (not separated here) far more than with the
+tone compared. In Experiment~1 the
+chunk-average reading (user, assistant and probe tokens mixed) falls from
+$+4.75$ and $+4.22$ (cheerful, hostile) at one emotional turn to $+0.44$
+and $-0.34$ at eight, against a gap of $0.53$ to $0.78$ between arms.
+The stored assistant turns are the model's planning text, which often names the
+user's tone (in Experiment~1, 59 of 135 hostile-arm and 54 of 135
+cheerful-arm excerpts), so readings of history contain the model's own
+written labels, except where the chat template stripped the planning.
+Experiment~1 cannot test accumulation: its arms differ in text at every
+read site. Experiment~2's arms differ in text at the measured turn, and
+its comparisons between them are withdrawn. The one length-matched
+comparison, hostile against cheerful persona prompts, moves valence
+($d_{\mathrm{av}}{=}-0.68$) consistently on five probes, too few to
+reach $p{<}0.05$ by permutation; its script is not shipped. A test
+would need fixed (teacher-forced) neutral assistant turns, the recurrent
+layers read turn by turn, position-matched or de-rotated keys, and
+behavior as ground truth.
